# Home context: one window per project I work at home.
#
# Format: <window-name> <directory>.  `~' is expanded.  A directory that does not
# exist on this machine falls back to $HOME.
#
# Read by tmux/pick-layout.sh in the work overlay, which runs from the
# `after-new-session' hook: a session named `home' gets this list, and so does any
# machine not on that script's work-host allowlist.  `tmux new -s work' forces the
# work list instead.  Read as DATA, not sourced by tmux -- hence `.list' and not
# `.conf', which tmux.conf's overlay glob would pick up.
#
# mirv, dartt and freebook are not cloned on either work machine, so those three
# fall back to $HOME there until they are.  The watcher dirs are VM-only, so they
# fall back too.
#
# The six shared with the work list -- quite, prevue-gaffer, gazette, dotfiles,
# watch-queue, skills-sync -- keep the same names AND the same directories in both
# contexts deliberately, so a window name means the same project and lands in the
# same tree whichever list built it.
#
# prevue-gaffer is one window because the pair is worked together, which is also
# why memory-personal/SLUGS.map gives either project's slug both memories.
mirv            ~/projects/mirv
dartt           ~/projects/dartt
freebook        ~/projects/freebook
quite           ~/projects/quite
prevue-gaffer   ~/projects/prevue
gazette         ~/projects/gazette
dotfiles        ~/lib/dotfiles
watch-queue     ~/lib/claude-watchers/watch-queue
skills-sync     ~/lib/claude-watchers/skills-sync
